{{!-- The left rail of a TABBED sheet — the standing rosters (the Judge's brands and oaths, the
      Blessed's marks), which show one list at a time so a window never grows taller than the
      screen.

      THE SIBLING OF guide-toc.hbs, AND NOT INTERCHANGEABLE WITH IT. That one drives a linear
      walkthrough and emits `data-step-index`, read by a stepper's `_goTo`; this one names its
      panels and emits `data-tab`, read by `applyGuideRail` with `dataKey: "tab"`. Routing one
      through the other's handler binds nothing and throws nothing, so the two are kept apart by
      emitting different attributes rather than by a comment asking nicely.

      Context:
        tabs      {key, title, icon, count, selected} in rail order
        ariaLabel what the rail is a list OF, for a screen reader
        btnClass  the host dialog's own class on each button, which it binds its click to

      `count` is how many rows the panel behind this entry holds. It rides the rail because the
      one thing worth knowing about a list you are NOT looking at is whether there is anything on
      it: a Blessed clicking through five kinds to find which ones have marks out would be worse
      served by the rail than by the stack it replaced. Suppressed at zero by the caller, which
      passes no count at all for an empty list. --}}
<nav class="stonetop-guide-toc" aria-label="{{ariaLabel}}">
	<ol class="stonetop-guide-toc-list">
		{{#each tabs}}
		<li class="stonetop-guide-toc-item{{#if selected}} is-active{{/if}}">
			<button type="button" class="stonetop-guide-toc-btn {{../btnClass}}" data-tab="{{key}}"{{#if selected}} aria-current="true"{{/if}}>
				<i class="fas {{icon}} stonetop-guide-toc-icon"></i>
				<span class="stonetop-guide-toc-text">{{title}}</span>
				{{#if count}}<span class="stonetop-guide-toc-count">{{count}}</span>{{/if}}
			</button>
		</li>
		{{/each}}
	</ol>
</nav>
